<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geghard</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Geghard is a medieval monastery in the Kotayk province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, partly carved out of the cliffs of the Azat river gorge. Founded according to tradition in the 4th century, its main church dates from 1215, built under the Zakarid princes, and the Proshyan family later added rock-hewn chambers. It is named after the spear (geghard) said to have pierced Christ, once kept there, and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geghard.jpg" alt="Geghard" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monasterio_de_Geghard,_Armenia,_2016-10-02,_DD_63.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to tradition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-the-illuminator">Gregory the Illuminator</a> founded the monastery in the 4th century at a spring inside a cave that had been sacred in pre-Christian times. For that reason it was first called Ayrivank, the Monastery of the Cave. Nothing of the first buildings has survived. Armenian historians of the 4th, 8th and 10th centuries describe religious buildings together with residential and service structures. The first monastery was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in the 9th century, and in 923 Nasr, a vice-regent of an Arabian caliph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, plundered its property, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and burned its buildings. Earthquakes also damaged it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The main church, the Katoghike, was built in 1215 under the brothers Zakare and Ivane, generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. It is built against the mountain, with an equal-armed cross plan inscribed in a square under a dome. West of it stands the gavit, built between 1215 and 1225, whose central dome of stalactite vaulting is the best example of the technique in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Ramparts of the 12th and 13th centuries protect three sides of the complex, and the cliffs the fourth.</p><p>After Prince Prosh Khaghbakian, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Zakarians, bought the monastery, the Proshyans made it a family monastery and mausoleum. Rock-cut chambers date from the 1240s onward: the first cave church, with a spring, in the 1240s; the second cave church and a sepulchre in 1283; and the upper zhamatun, the tomb of Papak Proshyan and his wife Ruzukan, in 1288. An inscription names the architect Galdzak. The Proshyans also provided an irrigation system, and the monastery was known as the Monastery of the Seven Churches and of the Forty Altars. Crosses carved in relief, khachkars, cover many of its rock faces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The name Geghardavank, the Monastery of the Spear, is first recorded in a document of 1250. It comes from the spear that wounded Christ at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crucifixion">Crucifixion</a>, allegedly brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> by the Apostle Thaddeus. A case was made for it in 1687, and it is now in the treasury at Echmiadzin. Relics of the Apostles Andrew and John were donated in the 12th century, and visitors made grants of land, money and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> over the centuries. The historian Mkhitar Ayrivanetsi lived in one of the cave cells in the 13th century.</p><p>The monastery later fell out of use, and its main church sheltered the winter flocks of Karapapakh nomads until a few monks from Ejmiatsin resettled it after the Russian conquest. It is listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and remains a place of pilgrimage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Geghard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A wave of building produced monasteries and churches at Harichavank, Haghartsin, <b>Geghard</b> and Ani.</blockquote><a href="/state/zakarid-armenia">Zakarid Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
